Secret settings cannot be saved from this UI as written: `PATCH /config/plugin-settings` defaults `scope` to "project", and the backend rejects saving `type: "secret"` keys to project scope. When the user edits a secret field, `handleSave()` includes it in the payload, which will trigger a 400. The save logic needs to either (a) send `scope: "global"` when any secret keys are present, or (b) split saves by scope (global for secrets, project for others) / offer a scope selector.
```suggestion
      const payload: Record<string, unknown> = {}
      let hasSecretInPayload = false
      for (const [key, def] of Object.entries(props.schema.properties)) {
        if (def.type === "secret") {
          if (state.touched.has(key)) {
            payload[key] = state.values[key]
            hasSecretInPayload = true
          }
        } else {
          payload[key] = state.values[key]
        }
      }

      if (hasSecretInPayload) {
        payload.scope = "global"
      }
```

The login page always redirects to "/" after a successful login, ignoring the `redirect` query parameter that was set in `wrappedFetch`. When a user is redirected from a protected page to `/login?redirect=/some/path`, the login page doesn't read that parameter, so the user will always end up at "/" after logging in, losing their original navigation target. The login handler should read the `redirect` query parameter from the URL and navigate to that path after a successful login, with validation to ensure the redirect target is a same-origin relative path to prevent open redirect attacks.
```suggestion
    const params = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search)
    const redirectParam = params.get("redirect")

    let target = "/"
    if (
      redirectParam &&
      redirectParam.startsWith("/") &&
      !redirectParam.startsWith("//")
    ) {
      target = redirectParam
    }

    window.location.href = target
```

The HMAC signature is compared using a plain string equality operator (`===`), which is not constant-time and could potentially be vulnerable to timing attacks. For a remotely accessible server, an attacker could exploit timing differences to forge a valid token. A constant-time comparison (using `crypto.subtle.timingSafeEqual`) should be used instead to prevent this class of attack.
```suggestion
  const expectedBytes = encoder.encode(expected)
  const tokenBytes = encoder.encode(token)
  if (expectedBytes.length !== tokenBytes.length) return null
  const isValid = await crypto.subtle.timingSafeEqual(expectedBytes, tokenBytes)
  if (!isValid) return null
```

