The `"close"` event handler on a connecting socket (before it opens) only clears `ws` if `ws === sock`, but during the connecting phase `ws` is still `undefined` (it is only set in the `"open"` handler). If the underlying socket fires `"close"` without first firing `"error"` (which is possible in some network conditions with the `ws` library), the `con` promise will never be settled — neither resolved nor rejected — and `con` will remain set to the hanging promise. Any subsequent call to `open()` where `!busy` will then return this unsettled promise, permanently blocking all future streaming requests over this instance.
```suggestion
        const isCurrentSocket = ws === sock
        if (isCurrentSocket) ws = undefined
        // If the connection promise is still pending when the socket closes,
        // reject it so callers don't get a never-settled promise.
        if (con) {
          const err = new Error("WebSocket connection closed before it was established")
          con = undefined
          log.warn("connect_closed_before_ready", { error: err, scope })
          reject(err)
          return
        }
```

The `msg` handler only closes the stream when the event type is `"response.completed"` or `"error"`. However, in the OpenAI Responses API WebSocket protocol (confirmed by the `openai-responses-language-model.ts` schema at line 1342), `"response.incomplete"` is also a terminal event that signals the response has ended without completion (e.g., due to token limits). Failing to treat `"response.incomplete"` as terminal here means the stream will never be closed in that case, the `busy` flag will remain `true` indefinitely, and subsequent requests over the same connection will be blocked or will create extra connections.
