`base()` is used for the blacklist check, but on Windows `path.win32.basename(file, ".exe")` only strips the extension if the casing matches exactly, and it preserves casing of the name. If `SHELL` is something like `Nu.EXE` or `NU.EXE`, `base()` won’t produce `"nu"`, so the blacklist won’t apply. Consider using `path.win32.parse(file).name.toLowerCase()` (or lowercasing the `basename` result) to make the check robust.
```suggestion
    if (process.platform === "win32") {
      // Use parse().name to strip the extension in a case-insensitive way, then normalize to lowercase
      return path.win32.parse(file).name.toLowerCase()
    }
    // On non-Windows, just take the basename and normalize to lowercase
    return path.basename(file).toLowerCase()
```

**`brew --repo` failure silently skips git pull, upgrading with a stale tap**

If `brew --repo anomalyco/tap` exits non-zero, `result.stdout` is empty, so `repo` becomes `""` after `.trim()`. The `if (repo)` guard is falsy, the pull is silently skipped, and execution falls through to `brew upgrade formula`. This means the tap formula could be stale — the original `&&`-chained shell command would have failed here (because `cd "$(brew --repo …)"` on an empty string exits non-zero, aborting the chain).

At minimum the exit code should be checked and surfaced, or the skip should be logged:

```ts
const repoResult = await Process.text(["brew", "--repo", "anomalyco/tap"], { env, nothrow: true })
if (repoResult.code !== 0) {
  result = repoResult
  break
}
const repo = repoResult.text.trim()
if (repo) {
  const pull = await Process.run(["git", "pull", "--ff-only"], { cwd: repo, env, nothrow: true })
  if (pull.code !== 0) {
    result = pull
    break
  }
}
```

`upgradeCurl()` can throw (e.g. network failure, `bash` missing/spawn error, stream read error). Unlike the other branches (which use `Process.run(..., { nothrow: true })`), these throws bypass the `result.code` handling and won’t be wrapped into `UpgradeFailedError`. Consider catching inside `upgradeCurl` (or around the call site) and returning a `{ code: 1, stdout, stderr }`-shaped result (or rethrowing as `UpgradeFailedError`) to keep error handling consistent across methods.
```suggestion
    try {
      const body = await fetch("https://opencode.ai/install").then((res) => {
        if (!res.ok) throw new Error(res.statusText)
        return res.text()
      })
      const proc = Process.spawn(["bash"], {
        stdin: "pipe",
        stdout: "pipe",
        stderr: "pipe",
        env: {
          ...process.env,
          VERSION: target,
        },
      })
      if (!proc.stdin || !proc.stdout || !proc.stderr) throw new Error("Process output not available")
      proc.stdin.end(body)
      const [code, stdout, stderr] = await Promise.all([proc.exited, buffer(proc.stdout), buffer(proc.stderr)])
      return {
        code,
        stdout,
        stderr,
      }
    } catch (error) {
      const message = error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)
      return {
        code: 1,
        stdout: Buffer.from(""),
        stderr: Buffer.from(message),
      }
```
